EGlib Unit Testing
===============================================================================

	1. Writing new tests
	2. Running them

===============================================================================
1. Writing new tests
===============================================================================

Tests are gtest cases, grouped by the API they cover: the GPtrArray cases are
in ptrarray.cpp and share the `ptrarray` suite name, and so on.  A file looks
like:

	#include <glib.h>
	#include <gtest/gtest.h>

	TEST (groupname, what_it_checks)
	{
		ASSERT_EQ (expected, g_something (input));
	}

Two things to know before naming a case:

  * eglib-remap.h rewrites the public names to their monoeg_ spellings, and
    gtest expands the name it is handed before stringizing it.  `TEST (strutil,
    g_strsplit)` therefore registers as `strutil.monoeg_g_strsplit`.  Leave the
    g_ off.

  * A case that needs process-wide state -- the working directory, an
    environment variable -- has to put it back, whether it passes or not.  See
    the fixture at the top of path.cpp; gtest is free to run these in any
    order, and `--gtest_shuffle` will.

Add the file to test-eglib's source list in CMakeLists.txt.  Nothing else
registers it: gtest_discover_tests runs the binary and picks up whatever is
there.

===============================================================================
2. Running them
===============================================================================

	ctest --test-dir <build> -L eglib

Every case is its own ctest test, named eglib/<suite>.<case>, so a failure
names exactly what broke and `ctest -R` reaches one of them.

The binary is also runnable on its own, from <build>/mono/eglib/test.  It has
to run from there: path/find_program_in_path_cwd blanks $PATH and then looks
for the test binary itself in the working directory.

	./test-eglib                              everything
	./test-eglib --gtest_filter='ptrarray.*'  one suite
	./test-eglib --gtest_shuffle              in a random order
	./test-eglib --gtest_repeat=100           a hundred times over

The UTF-* fixture files next to this README are read at run time.  They are
found through the source directory baked in at configure time; setting $srcdir
points the suite at a different copy.
